# Chapter Fourteen — The Second Train

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.1. Sunday. The whole table is in one room again before the end. The recovered packet is read to the people it names, in Nell's hand. Ruth offers Henry the third account. The working crime engine remains unratified. No interaction branches yet.

## Scene 42 — In her own hand

= Present: Ward, Nell, Henry, in an interview room at headquarters.

= Immediate pressure: Ward will read Nell the packet because she is the rights holder it names. He will not let her take it. She has a pencil.

INT. POLICE HEADQUARTERS, INTERVIEW ROOM - MORNING

Sunday morning the interview corridor is empty except for the clock, still four minutes fast. Ward has the packet on the table in its bags, one sheet to a bag, laid out in the order they were found. He reads each one through the plastic without lifting it.

Nell writes as he reads. She has a school notebook with a green cover and a pencil Ward gave her from his own pocket after she asked for one. Henry sits at the end of the table where Ward put him, with nothing in front of him.

Ward finishes the last bag.

WARD
"P. Ellery. Wednesday." Then the date. That is all of it.

Nell writes the date. She turns back through what she has written and counts the pages with the side of her thumb.

NELL
Eleven.

WARD
Seventeen of his. One half. The bundle underneath is the same text, typed earlier. He revised it on the loose sheets. The bundle is the draft.

NELL
You read me the loose sheets.

WARD
You asked for what he meant to hand over. That is what he meant to hand over.

Nell closes the notebook.

NELL
The letter is separate.

WARD
The letter is yours. It was addressed. This was not addressed. This was for a production office and a lawyer and me. He wrote it for people who would read it badly.

NELL
I am going to read it once to people who will read it worse. Then it can be evidence.

Ward looks at the green notebook.

WARD
Where?

NELL
Tolland's. Tonight. Edwin has agreed.

WARD
On a Sunday.

NELL
He said the Winter Room does not open on Sunday. I said it did not open on Wednesday.

WARD
Who?

NELL
Everyone he wrote down.

WARD
Ellery.

NELL
She is written down.

Ward does not answer at once. He puts the bags back into their envelope in order.

WARD
It is your property and it is your hand. I cannot stop you reading your own handwriting to your friends. I can tell you that Miss Ellery was released last night at ten, that she is not to leave the county, and that anything she says at your table is something Calder will be asked to repeat.

HENRY
I know.

WARD
I did not say it to you.

He stands.

WARD
Is there soup?

NELL
I don't know.

WARD
Holt's statement had the soup in it. Two pages. I have read it three times and I still do not know what he thought of it.

He opens the door for them.

WARD
Calder.

Henry stops.

WARD
You have something.

HENRY
Not yet.

WARD
That is not what I asked.

HENRY
I have something I have not decided about.

WARD
Then don't decide. Call me.

HENRY
Tomorrow.

Ward looks at him the way he looked at Nell on the gallery.

WARD
Tomorrow is a day I have.

He goes back down the corridor. Nell puts the pencil in her handbag with the notebook, then takes it out and lays it on the bench outside the door, because it is his.

## Scene 43 — Wilshire

= Present: Henry and Nell, in Henry's car.

= Immediate pressure: Nell has eleven pages in her handbag and forty minutes of boulevard between her apartment and the people they name. Henry has one night left before he calls Ward.

INT. HENRY'S CAR, WILSHIRE BOULEVARD - EVENING

At twenty past seven, Henry's car turns onto Wilshire heading east. The boulevard is six lanes wide and nearly empty, and its signals are timed so that a car at thirty-five never has to stop. Henry drives at thirty-five.

The stores along the boulevard are closed and their windows are lit. Mannequins in fur stand in October. A woman made of plaster holds a telephone she will never put down. Beyond them the sales floors are dark, the way Tolland's was dark on Wednesday.

Nell watches the windows go by.

NELL
They leave the lights on for nobody.

HENRY
For the car.

NELL
For the car.

A billboard passes over them. An actress's face, forty feet across, opening Friday. Nell does not look up at it. Henry does, and then looks back at the road.

Palms stand along the median in the sodium light, lit from beneath and black above. A gas station is open, its attendant sitting on an oil drum. A bus bench advertises a funeral home. On the seat, a boy of about fifteen is asleep with his shoes off and his shoes beside him, in a row.

HENRY
He put the screws in a row.

NELL
What?

HENRY
Paul. The fan at the Kenton Rooms. He took it apart and left the pieces in the order they went back.

NELL
He did that with everything.

She looks at her handbag.

NELL
He did it with this.

Henry drives.

NELL
You asked what I would recognize. Her handwriting, her voice. I said the things we kept. I did not say I would recognize him. I recognized him in every line of it. He made a list. He put down what he said to each of us and what was true, one under the other, as if somebody could come along after and put it back together from the order.

HENRY
Somebody can.

NELL
Not the part that matters.

They pass a hospital, its windows in rows.

NELL
She died in there. Not this one. One like it. In December, fourteen years ago. The picture had stopped in March. She spent the months between telling all of us it was not over. Her heart. Nobody had told her about her heart. She was forty-one.

HENRY
Who was with her?

NELL
Marian. Lydia was at the store. Robert drove me from school and I was too late and he apologized for the traffic. He still apologizes for the traffic.

She puts her hand flat on the handbag.

NELL
I am going to read it kindly. Once. Then Ward can have it.

HENRY
You said they would read it worse.

NELL
Yes. So it has to be me.

The lake at Westlake comes up on the right, black, with the lit path around it and nobody on the path. Lydia's building is two streets north. Her window is dark. She is already at Tolland's, being early.

NELL
Marian said I should not have asked her.

HENRY
Ruth.

NELL
Yes.

HENRY
Why did you?

NELL
Because he wrote her down. He wrote all of us down, and she is the one he wrote most carefully.

She turns in her seat to look at him.

NELL
You said you would tell me when it mattered.

HENRY
I did.

NELL
Does it?

Henry keeps his eyes on the road.

HENRY
There is something she told me in the car on Thursday. I have not told Ward. I have not told you.

NELL
Why not?

HENRY
Because I have not decided what it means, and he told me to call him before I decide.

NELL
Then call him.

HENRY
Tomorrow.

NELL
You are giving her one more night.

HENRY
I am giving myself one.

NELL
I lied to Ward on Wednesday night. I took it back on Thursday morning, before anybody had gone looking. Nothing of mine has had to be found. Everything of hers has.

HENRY
Yes.

Nell looks at him a while longer, then turns to the windshield.

NELL
That is the first thing you have told me badly.

Wilshire ends. The streets narrow and the buildings close in and become downtown, and Tolland's is ahead of them, a dark block with its top floor lit. From the car the city is not a field of lights. It is a street with people on it, and tonight, for once, it has an audience.

Henry turns onto Hill.

## Scene 44 — Eight

= Present: Henry, Nell, Lydia, Marian, Robert, Edwin, Ruth, Bell.

= Immediate pressure: everyone Paul wrote down is at one table, and the person reading is the one he wrote it for. Ruth has come because she was named.

INT. THE WINTER ROOM - NIGHT

Bell opens the Hill Street door. He has his cap on. He locks the door behind them and takes them up in the service lift without the brass key, which is in an envelope at headquarters.

The Winter Room is lit the way it was on Wednesday. One round table stands at the center under the amber lamps. It is laid for eight.

Lydia is there, sitting where she sat. There are no place cards. Marian and Robert stand at the windows with their coats still on. Edwin comes out of the kitchen in his working suit with a cloth over his arm.

LYDIA
You're late.

NELL
By a minute.

LYDIA
For a second time, that is considered forgiveness.

EDWIN
Miss Shaw is quoting me. Badly.

Marian comes from the window and takes Nell's face in both hands and looks at it, then lets go. Robert takes Nell's coat, then Henry's, and stands holding both until Edwin takes them from him.

ROBERT
I'll put them—

EDWIN
I will.

ROBERT
Thank you. I don't know where anything is tonight.

MARIAN
You never do.

She says it and then puts her hand on his sleeve, and he looks down at the hand, and neither of them takes it back.

The lift bell sounds below.

Lydia looks toward the vestibule and does not look away from it.

The lift opens. Ruth steps out alone. She is in the coat she wore to the police station. She stops at the edge of the light.

LYDIA
You came alone.

RUTH
You told me not to, once.

LYDIA
That was Wednesday.

RUTH
Yes.

Nell crosses the room to her. She does not embrace her. She stands close enough to speak quietly and speaks at ordinary volume.

NELL
You are written down. Sit where you sat.

RUTH
Beside Henry.

NELL
Beside Henry.

Ruth goes to the chair. She does not take off her coat until she is sitting in it, and then she does, and hangs it on the chair back, and puts her hands in her lap.

Edwin looks at the eighth place.

EDWIN
Mr. Bell.

BELL
Sir?

EDWIN
Sit.

BELL
I'm on, sir.

EDWIN
You are on. Sit.

BELL
Where would I—

EDWIN
Where the chair is.

Bell looks at the chair across from Nell. He looks at it for a while. Then he takes off his cap and sits with the cap on his knee and his back not touching the chair.

BELL
I'd have rather not, sir.

EDWIN
I know.

Edwin goes into the kitchen and comes out with a platter, and the platter is cold chicken, cut up, and bread, and a dish of salt.

He sets it down in the center of the table.

Robert looks at the chicken. Everyone at the table has heard him say the same thing about chicken for thirty years, and everyone waits for it, and Robert looks at the chicken and says nothing.

Marian puts her hand on his arm for that.

NELL
Eat the middle.

Lydia closes her eyes and opens them.

They eat. Nobody has requested anything, and Edwin puts a glass in front of each of them, and each glass is the right one. Bell's is water. He drinks half of it at once and puts it down carefully as if it might be counted.

Nell takes the green notebook from her handbag and puts it beside her plate.

NELL
Detective Ward read me what Paul brought on Wednesday. Not the letter. The other thing. I copied it. This is my hand, not his. He wrote it to be read by people who would read it badly, and I am going to read it once here, and then it can be evidence.

MARIAN
Why here?

NELL
Because it has all of you in it and Lydia has a train.

ROBERT
Monday.

LYDIA
Ten past eight.

NELL
I will read each of you the part with your name. You may say one thing after. Then I go on.

MARIAN
And if we—

NELL
One thing.

Marian stops.

Nell opens the notebook. She reads the heading in a level voice.

NELL
"Corrected declaration. Four eighteen R. P. Ellery. I withdraw 'through family.' The authority was never mine. The material is June Avery's. Her daughter, Nell Avery, is the person the production should have asked. What follows is what I told each person, and what was true."

She turns the page.

NELL
"Miss Shaw. Mr. Holt gave her my number. She telephoned and asked me to bring the material to a supper at Tolland's on Wednesday. I asked her to tell Nell first. She said Nell would not come if she knew. I said then don't tell her. She didn't, and my sister was not told either. I agreed to that. I should not have. It was the same as every arrangement I have made in four months: everybody in a separate room, and me carrying the pages between them."

Nell looks up.

Lydia has her hands flat on the tablecloth.

LYDIA
I had a speech for Wednesday. It began with June, and it went through all of you in order, and it was going to end with the train, and it was—

NELL
One thing.

Lydia stops. She looks at her hands.

LYDIA
He asked me to tell you. I said no. That is the thing.

NELL
It is.

She turns the page.

NELL
"Mr. Holt. Came twice. The first time with a list in Miss Shaw's hand. The second time he asked whether I meant to go through with it. He said Mrs. Holt wanted the box destroyed and had said so to Miss Shaw twice, and that he had told Mrs. Holt he was helping Lydia pack, which was true, and had not told her the rest. He asked me not to say he had told me. He also said that if I did not bring the box on Wednesday, he would tell Nell himself where it was. I believed him. I put it down because I said I would leave nothing out, and because it is the bravest thing anyone has said to me this year."

Nobody looks at Robert. Then Marian does.

MARIAN
You would have told her.

ROBERT
Yes.

MARIAN
You never told me that.

ROBERT
You'd have told me not to.

MARIAN
Yes.

ROBERT
That's why.

Marian opens her mouth to correct something. For the first time in Henry's hearing there is nothing in the sentence to correct, and she closes it again.

MARIAN
Yes, Robert.

It is what she said on Wednesday when he came in late. It does not mean the same thing.

Nell turns the page.

NELL
"Mr. Price. I have not met him. Miss Shaw told me she had taken her own record out of the store under a description a friend wrote for her, so that nobody at the dock would look inside. I put it here because I did the same thing with a sound house and two receipts, and I am not in a position to call it anything but what it is."

Edwin stands very straight beside the service bar.

EDWIN
He was not wrong.

He waits, and there is more, and he cannot find where it begins.

EDWIN
In twenty-six years she has asked me for four—

He stops.

EDWIN
I have said this to the detective. I have nothing to add to it that would be true.

NELL
That is one thing.

EDWIN
It is.

Nell turns the page. She reads more slowly.

NELL
"Nell. I telephoned her at a quarter to six on Wednesday. I asked if she would listen after supper. She said yes, if I spoke first, and did not stop halfway and wait for somebody to make the rest bearable. I said all right. I have typed this before supper so that if I stop halfway, there is a record that I knew the condition, and agreed to it."

She closes her hand on the edge of the page.

NELL
I have nothing to say after mine.

Bell puts his cap on the table beside his plate and then takes it back onto his knee.

BELL
There's nothing with me in it, miss.

NELL
No. You held the box.

BELL
I held the box.

He looks at the platter.

BELL
I've thought about it. I've thought about it a good deal. I had the desk in my ear. Four minutes. The desk reads back every door, so it's four. And I could have heard that bell through it. I've tested it now, I've stood there with the telephone and had it rung. I'd have heard it. I keep going over the four minutes and there wasn't anything. There wasn't anything to hear. That's what I keep coming to. There wasn't a bell.

Nobody looks at Ruth. That is how Henry knows that every person at the table has already thought it.

Ruth looks at her plate.

Nell turns the last page and does not read it. She closes the notebook on her finger and holds it out across Henry to Ruth.

NELL
Yours. Read it.

Ruth takes the notebook. She opens it where Nell's finger was. She reads Paul's words in Nell's hand, and she reads them without expression, the way she would read copy back to a client.

RUTH
"R.E. is my sister, Ruth Ellery. I brought her pages without a title and paid her less than the production paid me. On the third set she asked the title and I said it would change. On the fifth she asked in writing whether it was a private recording, and I wrote that it was rehearsal and the family had cleared it. The family had not cleared it. Nell had not been asked. My sister asked once, and I answered so that she would not ask again, and she did not ask again. The answer is mine. The not asking again is hers. I have attached the page so that both are in one place."

Ruth closes the notebook and puts it on the tablecloth between herself and Nell.

Nell looks at it.

NELL
He never wrote like that.

RUTH
No.

NELL
How much of it was yours?

RUTH
None.

The room is quiet.

Nell takes the notebook back and puts it in her handbag.

NELL
Mr. Bell. You told Ward he said, "That's the only part I wrote."

BELL
Yes, miss. About the sheet with the pencil. "That's the only part I wrote." I took it to mean the pencil.

NELL
It was the only thing in the carton he had made up himself.

She looks around the table.

NELL
That is all of it. It is evidence now.

Edwin lifts his glass. Everyone waits.

EDWIN
Miss Shaw requested no speeches.

LYDIA
I didn't.

EDWIN
Then I am requesting one.

He holds the glass a moment longer, and lowers it without drinking, and sits.

Robert reaches over and moves the salt in front of him, because Marian has not.

LYDIA
Ruth.

Ruth looks up.

LYDIA
I was going to forgive you on Wednesday.

RUTH
I know. You told me on the telephone.

LYDIA
For the sentence. The one you gave him for leaving her. I was going to do it at the table with everybody there, so that it would count.

RUTH
In front of everybody. Yes.

LYDIA
It has gotten larger.

RUTH
Yes.

LYDIA
I can't now. I don't know how it's done at this size.

RUTH
Nobody asked you to.

Lydia nods. It is not agreement.

MARIAN
Ten past eight.

LYDIA
Union Station. The day coach. I am not going to be seen off.

NELL
No.

LYDIA
Good.

NELL
Mr. Calder will see you onto it.

Lydia looks at Henry. So does Ruth.

LYDIA
For what?

NELL
I am paying him for the attempt.

Henry says yes.

Edwin goes to the wall and opens one panel of the glass roof with the long pole. The night air comes in the way it did on Wednesday, and it is colder, because the week has moved.

Marian stands. Robert stands with her. He looks for the coats and Edwin already has them.

Marian goes around the table to Nell and does not touch her.

MARIAN
Come to the house.

NELL
Tomorrow.

MARIAN
Tonight.

NELL
Tomorrow, Marian.

Marian accepts it the way she accepted the cinnamon.

Lydia rises. She stops beside Bell, who has stood up because everyone has.

LYDIA
Mr. Bell. Thank you for the water.

BELL
It was only water, miss.

LYDIA
I know what it was.

She goes to the lift with the Holts. Bell goes with them, because the lift is his, and puts his cap on in the vestibule.

Nell stops at Henry's chair.

NELL
Tomorrow you call him.

HENRY
Yes.

NELL
Then you see her onto a train.

HENRY
Ten past eight.

NELL
Then you come and tell me badly.

HENRY
Yes.

She looks at Ruth, who has not stood.

NELL
Good night, Ruth.

RUTH
Good night.

Nell goes. The lift takes her down. Edwin, at the kitchen door, looks at the open roof and then at the two of them, and goes into the kitchen, and the door swings and stops.

## Scene 45 — The third account

= Present: Ruth and Henry, under the open roof panel.

= Immediate pressure: Ruth has one version left that lets her keep him. She has had a night at home to write it.

INT. THE WINTER ROOM - NIGHT

Ruth stays in her chair. Henry stays in his. The table between them is what is left of a dinner: the platter, the salt, eight glasses at eight different levels.

Beyond the windows the city is a field of lights. Tonight it has an audience. Neither of them is looking at it.

RUTH
Was that kind?

HENRY
She said once.

RUTH
She did it once.

Ruth turns her glass a quarter turn on the cloth.

RUTH
I went up because I heard paper tear.

Henry waits.

RUTH
That part was true on Friday and it is true now. He set the carton down and opened it and I saw the cover, my cover, with his pencil on it, on top of a bundle, and I said, so that is what you are correcting, and he picked the whole thing up and went up the stair with it. To get away from Bell. To get away from me. I stood in the vestibule. I heard paper tear. I opened the door.

HENRY
And went up.

RUTH
And went up. And he was on the lip.

She says it plainly, without adjusting it.

RUTH
Not over. On it. His hands were on the steel and his feet were on the wall. I could see his hands. They were white. I got down on the planks and I had his wrist.

HENRY
Which hand?

RUTH
Right. His right, my right. I had it in both of mine and I had my knees against the post at the end of the rail and I could not lift him. He is heavier than me. Was. I could hold him. I could not lift him.

HENRY
How long?

RUTH
I don't know. Long enough for my hands to hurt. Long enough to know I could hold him or I could hold—

She stops.

The sentence has a second half and she has heard what it is before she reached it. She does not say it.

RUTH
Or I could not.

HENRY
Or you could what?

RUTH
Nothing. Or I could not. That's all. I could hold him and not lift him, and then I could not hold him.

She looks at her hands on the cloth.

RUTH
He went through the moon. I heard him land. I did not look. Then I looked. The paper was in the way and then it was not. And there were pages all around my knees, and I did the one thing on that gallery that had an order to it. Paper goes in a pile. I put the paper in a pile. I put the pile in my bag. I went down and got into the lift and gave Edwin his key and said not to wait, because if I had said one more word than that I would have said all of it, and I did not know yet what all of it was.

Henry does not move.

RUTH
That is what happened. I did not tell Ward, because Ward wants the rail, and this is not the rail. This is a woman on her knees who was not strong enough. He does not have a form for that.

HENRY
What did Paul say?

RUTH
When?

HENRY
On the lip.

RUTH
Nothing. There wasn't time for anything. He was breathing. I was breathing. It was very loud. Nobody said anything.

Henry hears Thursday's car. Alvarado, the sprinklers on in front of the bank, the freeway loud enough that neither of them had to speak. "Help me." "And I said no."

He does not say it. He notices that he does not say it, and that a part of him is pleased to have somewhere to put it that is not on the table between them.

RUTH
You put your hand in the door.

HENRY
Yes.

RUTH
For him. A man you had never met. You put your hand in the door, and it opened, and he stayed. And that is the whole reason he was on that gallery.

HENRY
Yes.

RUTH
Ward asked you why.

HENRY
I said I didn't know.

RUTH
Neither do I.

She has made it sound alike. She has made it sound like the same not knowing. Henry hears her make it, and it is very well made.

RUTH
You could live with that.

Henry says nothing.

RUTH
It fits the stair. It fits the marks. It fits the whiting. It fits everything Ward walked us through on Friday night, and it fits what I told him inside, it only goes one step further into it. I was there. I had his wrist. I could not hold him. I picked up the paper because it was there.

HENRY
And the button.

RUTH
I did not see a button.

HENRY
You saw his hands were white.

Ruth looks at him.

For a moment there is an answer in her face, the kind she is good at. He watches her put it away.

RUTH
I saw his hands.

She stands. She takes her coat from the chair and does not put it on.

RUTH
I told Edwin you listen.

HENRY
I did.

RUTH
That isn't what I asked you for.

HENRY
I know what you asked me for.

She holds the coat against her a moment. Then she puts it on, one sleeve and then the other, without help, and buttons it.

RUTH
Tomorrow.

HENRY
Tomorrow.

RUTH
She said you'd tell her badly.

HENRY
I will.

RUTH
Tell me first.

He does not answer. She waits for the answer long enough to know he is not withholding it. He does not have it.

She goes to the lift. Bell has brought it back up. He does not look at her and he does not look away. The wire-glass door closes, and the floor numbers go down, and Henry watches them the way he watched them on Wednesday, all the way to three, and then past it.

Edwin comes out of the kitchen with the pole for the roof panel. He looks at Henry alone at a table laid for eight. He goes back into the kitchen and leaves the panel open.

The air that comes through it is Wednesday's air. It is colder. The week has moved, and the city goes on below with its audience, and Henry sits in it with the one thing he has not told anyone, and finds that he has already known since Thursday what it means. He had only wanted a night in which he had not decided.

It is over.
